Z||000011||pHoOl||08-30-2000||05:46 PM||||Alright, i figured it was too slow.  By the way, it's an auto, and it seems that from the time i floor it until it starts to move is about a second.  does this have something to do with the trans getting old?  I've heard that when autos get old they start hesitating like that.  I'm trying to figure out if there are any sort of cheap things that i might be able to do to get it to Mudstain Killer times on the 0-60.  I'll check oil and trans fluid today, but are there any other ideas?  i looked at the air filter, and it's clean.  HELP!!! MY GT IS SLOW!!!!!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Xtreme Fiero's||08-30-2000||06:01 PM||||My friend has a 88 auto GT w/ 120k miles when we went to the track for he ran a 19.99 @ under 75 mph  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> he was pissed. we gave it a basic tune up and and he ran a 16.1 a week later he replaced hs cloged cat. and fixed his TV cable. 15.9 a week later is trans went out on him si it might be faster now, He's got all B&m guts in it now. so i think you should start with a full tune up & check your cat & TV cable. I will pribably make a day/night change.||||reg||1
Z||000013||pHoOl||08-30-2000||06:16 PM||||yeah, i might take it to a local place and get a tune up.  I suggested it to my dad, but he told me that there isn't anything that they could tune up.  any other suggestions?  new spark plugs?  by the way, some times it sounds like one of the cylenders is missing a compression.  I'm not a mechanic, nor do i claim to know anything about engines, but the rumble sometimes has an off beat in it.  (can you tell i'm a musician?)  I'm sure that this would make a major difference, and if i can find a tape recorder here, i'll tape it and make it into a .wav so you guys can here it.  what all would make a cylinder?  Anything cheap to fix?  Thanks all for your quick responses!! you guys are the best<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||86SE||08-30-2000||06:25 PM||||Try running 92 octane with just a couple gallons in the tank.  Check your RPO codes on the front left fender under the hood, if you have a code 8PSC your final drive ratio is 2.84 which can cause slower acceleration, but if you have the code 8CPC you have a final drive of 3.33 which will bring quicker acceleration.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000015||pHoOl||08-30-2000||06:42 PM||||Two questions:<P>1.  I thought if an engine was designed to run on 87 octane, bumping up to 92 won't do anything performance wise.<P>2.  Why would they make two different gear ratios on GTs?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||86SE||08-30-2000||06:52 PM||||You would be surprised the performance you gain using 92 octane.  Not saying it will dramaticly increase performance for a Fiero, but for smaller engines, like ATV engines the increase in power is very very noticeable.<P>The 3.33 ratio is the performance GT (quicker acceleration) and the 2.84 would be for better gass mileage.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000017||pHoOl||08-30-2000||06:59 PM||||I know, but still, why did they make two different ratios?  was it an option, or a half year change or something?<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||vinnywolfe||08-30-2000||07:18 PM||||octane rating does nothing to improve the performance of the car.  the higher the octane rating, the slower the mass burn.<BR>in just about any stock car (with the exception of 'vettes, vipers and european sports cars) there is no need for it.  these high performance cars have higher compression ratios and more advanced timing.<BR>with the advance in timing, you want to slow the burn down so that it does not complete befor tdc.  of course if you have done mods to your fiero to affect factory timing, then maybe run higher octane, but with the stock fiero V6 there would be no advantage and actually, 87 octane,  has a quicker, complete burn, resulting in more of a "burst" of engergy compared to a slower combustion.<BR>the quicker burn unfortunately also results in more heat.  this is why you run higher octane fuel as a BANDAGE for preignition (spark knock).  the burn slows down and completes across the quench area rather than two quick flame fronts (resulting from excessive temps lighting off the air fuel mix along with the ignition spark) colliding and causing that "pinging" noise or spark knock.<BR>you're just wasting your money on higher octane gasoline with a STOCK fiero.<P>tomorrow lecture will be on flame-front propagation.<P>vinny||||reg||1
Z||000019||Xtreme Fiero's||08-30-2000||10:17 PM||||Look @ your air filter, not the out side like most cars but the inside. The V-6 fieros draw air up through the center of the filter. also try puting the car in 1st or 2dn while slowing down If your exhaust is recoiling badly you timing may be off or the cat may be semi cloged. you might have carbon in the rotor. a tuneup can't hurt it, the front 3 plugs may have never been changed you never know unless you got the car new or have had them canged yourself.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Shiner||08-30-2000||11:10 PM||||Vinny,<BR> Thanks.  You saved me ten minutes of typing on the octane topic   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> 86SE,<BR> I am sorry, I misread your comment!<p>[This message has been edited by Shiner (edited 08-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||86SE||08-30-2000||11:28 PM||||I know, thats why I said 60-0<P>And I can do that with my '86 Special Edition High Output V-6 Fiero with the Heavy Duty Special Suspension.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000022||Gold-86SE||08-31-2000||10:30 AM||||<BR>After reading all of that information, I can't remember if this has been mentioned but here is what I would do.<P>Do a major tuneup, to include the following items:<BR>replace spark plugs<BR>replace spark plug wires<BR>replace distributor cap and rotor<BR>replace pcv valve<BR>replace the fuel filter<BR>replace the air cleaner<BR>change the oil and filter<BR>change the belts on the engine<BR>check timing, should be set to 10 degrees tdc (top dead center)<P>All of these things can be done if you have tools and the desire, you don't have to be a mechanic.  For what you would spend to have a garage do the work for you, you could purchase the tools, supplies and a haynes manual to explain the procedures.  Then you have the satisfaction of working on the Fiero yourself and knowing it was done right.<P>Just post additional questions when they arise and the guys here will help.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000023||pHoOl||08-31-2000||02:34 PM||||for the guys with manuals... what RPM do you shift at for performance?  I thought i heard that five and a half was a good point, so while i was out today on some back roads, i put the car in the "1" automatic position, and hit the gas and the timer.  Shifted at 5500 and noticed a quicker time.  aparently the automatic shifts at 5000 when floored.<P>couldn't find the 8psc or 8cpc, all of mine are three character codes.  i'm assuming that you meant the "service parts, don't remove" if this isn't it, let me know.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||Carrolles||08-31-2000||03:20 PM||||I'd add a good de-carbonizing treatment to the tune-up.  Badly carboned up valves will restrict air/fuel flow into the cylinder.  Carbon on the piston tops will contribute to pre-ignition and poor air/fuel swirl.  Carbon in the throttle body will effect idle and smooth acceleration.||||reg||1
Z||000025||88formula||08-31-2000||06:32 PM||||If you put higher octane fuel than your car needs in it your car will actually run slower at he track because of the slower fuel burn rates of the higher octane fuel. If it improves your cars acceleration than the reason why is because there was abnormal combustion and the higher octane fuel stopped the abnormal combustion.||||reg||1
